hat's a project for the  future sometime so after graduate school  I was you know teaching in the prison  and I was also working on my own  scholarly work in my office on my own  and you know going to conferences and  engaging in in  in ours and writing papers and all that  sort of stuff and I didn't write much on  the Stoics but I was very interested as  I still am in models of practical  rationality and I've always used the  Stoics as one of those examples I also  became more and more interested in the  let's call it the phenomenon of anger  and the relationship between that  emotion the other emotions human drives  and desires and the Faculty of reason  and how anger influences our choices how  it directs in certain respects our will  so I began reading what different  philosophical and literary and  theological and psychological traditions  had to say about anger and the Stoics  have a lot to say about that topic that  I found quite interesting I don't agree  with all of it you know for example the  Stoics think that anger is uniformly bad  and the Aristotelian czar sort of  they're big you know rivals and they  they say that and so I don't go all the  way along with the Stoics on that but I  do think that the Stoics developed a lot  of really useful techniques so if you if  you were an Aristotelian and thought  that anger could be sometimes good but  was often bad and it needed to be you  know the emotions needed to be trained  and all that you you couldn't you know  do do worse than to bring in a whole  bunch of stoic insights and ideas and  that is in fact what I do in anger  management when I'm working with clients  I actually bring together these these  perspectives so you know doing all this  research on anger and how it's been  understood over the ages and what works  and what doesn't work involved a lot of  study of the Stoics and that got me  reading you know one of the other  important stomachs who we still have the  works of and that's Seneca because  Seneca wrote a book on anger and it also  got me to learn what other people had to  say  they say about the Stoics which is part  of how we learn about about the stoic  tradition by reading Galen for example  or by reading Plutarch or some of these  other authors who were not Stoics and  we're sometimes quite critical of the  Stoics Cicero's you know another another  person that I read quite a bit and so  what was great about this is it led to  me having gradually developing a deeper  understanding of the history of ideas  and its development in in the West you  know through the development of Greek  philosophy and its importation into the  Roman Empire and then it's spread and  and how it continued influencing things  during the Dark Ages and the  reconstitution and the Middle Ages and  you know the High Middle Ages and then  into the Renaissance in the modern  period so you know I was interested in  stoicism not so much just in terms of  you know philosophy as a way of life the  way that a lot of people are interested  in it by the way the philosophy is a way  of life thing you know great that that  people like to read pure I know that was  never something that people weren't  practicing in the past I mean you know  it's not a rediscovery to know that that  philosophy was meant to be practical in  a way of like read some Hegel read some  Maurice blown-down read some Kierkegaard  read some you know read these other  people read a piano song who Otto you  know took quite a quite a bit from and  you'll realize that this is this is not  a radically new conception but in any  case you know I was I was very  interested in how the Stoics fit into  things and then I you know there was a  back-and-forth I would see okay this is  useful for this idea this is useful for  the anger that I'm trying to make sense  of and also struggling with and and you  know might want to write about sometime  and these these inter fused with each  other so that took me  to quite a long time I ended up writing  an article for the Catholic the new  Catholic Encyclopedia on stoicism which  was cool because I got to do some more  research and learn more about the early  and and middle Stoics some of which I  already knew but a lot of which I got to  you know do some more you know study  about and then in the last five years I  would say I've become more and more  interested part you know in a  particularly personal way and I think  for myself it probably took a maturation  process before I could completely  understand why stoic philosophy would in  fact have the attraction that it does  you know I'm somebody who took quite a  while to grow up you might say and I  found stoic insights very useful in you  know my philosophical counseling  practice working with clients I you know  sometimes don't tell them that the ideas  are coming from the Stoics but I used  quite a quite a bit of it particularly  from epictetus I have to say he's  probably my favorite of the Stoics and  as you probably know I I'm actually at  present the editor of stoicism today  which is an online magazine let's call  it devoted to you know ways in which  people are adapting stoicism to to the  modern age and the problems of life I'm  very honored to have that position and  it came about because I was doing more  and more writing and more and more video  work dealing with the Stoics I still  have yet to do much academic writing but  that's you know slow in coming but I  will eventually be writing some articles  on the Stoics  I'm working on several different book  projects more popularly oriented having  to do with with stoicism and it's it's  you know relevance and addict  a Shinto to various issues like anger  for example or what stoicism has to say  about leadership and I I think that it's  going to be something that I'm going to  keep on researching and deepening my  engagement with over the course of my  career which I'm hoping is only half  over  you know if I live to 80 or so and get  to work you know the entire time I'll  have a lot more interesting things about  the Stoics towards the end of that then  I then I perhaps do at present but and  I'll hopefully also know a little bit  more experiential about putting the  stuff into practice but it's a something  that I'm very interested in and I  present doesn't mean that I'm not  equally within the Aristotelian  tradition or other important traditions  that are components of my philosophical  commitments and developments but  stoicism is something that I think for  me is here to stay
 

